onsider the Surrender is made. (ii) Surrender differs from abandonment. Abandonment of rights is simply an act on the part of a lessee/tenant. Surrender is a contractual act and occurs by mutual consent. Surrender must be reflected in a consensual act where as abandonment is a unilateral act on the part of the tenant. (iii) The act of the Respondent (tenant - signing such a deed) (as an attesting witness) is tantamount to a representation or holding out by the Respondent, that the Appellant obtains title free from tenancy rights which works an estoppel as against the Respondent (tenant)",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:485:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,"Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion","(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:486:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,"Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57","(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:487:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court,"(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1147:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A party cannot be inconsistent in their approach in legal proceedings, and they cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.","He cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion. The doctrine of approbate and reprobate forbids him from doing so.",SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1601:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A party cannot re-agitate an issue of prescription before a higher court if they failed to appeal against the judgment of the lower court on that issue.,"His contention, therefore, is that the Defendant cannot reagitate the issue of prescription before this Court.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1663:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A respondent who has not filed a written objection under Section 772 of the Civil Procedure Code may still be able to resist a decree of a lower Court in the interest of justice, even if the written objection has not been filed.","Hence, it is settled law that, even though a respondent-defendant who has not duly filed a written objection is unable to claim his statutory right to a decree as an entitlement, the Court sitting in appeal has the discretion, by virtue of its inherent jurisdiction, to permit such objection in the interest of justice.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1772:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"When interpreting a document, all clauses must be considered together, and not just one clause.",Clauses in a document must be interpreted in accordance with other clauses contained in the same document whether they precede or follow it.,SRC030-s147,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1849:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit.,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit [Muttu Natchia et al v. Patuma Natchia et al (1 NLR 21); Sundra Ammal v. Jusey Appu (36 NLR 400); Pedrick v. Mendis (62 NLR 471); K. Hassen v. A.O.Nagaria (75 NLR 335); Mansoor v. Umma (1984) 1 Sri LR 151; Subramaniam v. Pathmanathan (1984) 1 Sri LR 252; Ranasinghe v. Premadharma (1985) 1 Sri LR 63 at 71].,SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:1975:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A respondent to an appeal can resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds decided against him in the court below, without filing a written objection under section 772, but can also file a written objection to dispute specific findings in or aspects of the decree.","Thus, where an appellant from a decree entered by an original Civil Court has filed an appeal seeking to set aside or vary that decree: the first limb of section 772 recognises the right of a respondent to that appeal to resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds including those decided against him by the trial court, without filing a written objection under section 772; and the second limb of section 772 enables a respondent to the appeal who is dissatisfied with some specific finding in or aspect of the decree but has not filed an appeal to canvass it, to dispute that finding or aspect of the decree and seek to have it set aside or varied or decided in his favour by the appellate court, provided he has duly filed a written objection under section 772.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2027:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A party cannot file a re-vindicatio action against a co-owner without first obtaining a partition of the co-owned property.,Therefore there is no legal bar to allow the Plaintiff to proceed with the case as a re-vindicatio action against the Defendants.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:2113:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A defendant who fails to move the court to try an issue as a preliminary issue at the commencement of the trial is deemed to have waived objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of the court to hear and determine the defendant's action.,The defendant has failed to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue. The defendant has failed to formulate a preliminary issue relating to the jurisdiction of the Court at the commencement of the trial. His failure to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue on such a vital matter will amount to a waiver of objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of Court to hear and determine the defendant’s action.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
