scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:26:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
