ender is made. (ii) Surrender differs from abandonment. Abandonment of rights is simply an act on the part of a lessee/tenant. Surrender is a contractual act and occurs by mutual consent. Surrender must be reflected in a consensual act where as abandonment is a unilateral act on the part of the tenant. (iii) The act of the Respondent (tenant - signing such a deed) (as an attesting witness) is tantamount to a representation or holding out by the Respondent, that the Appellant obtains title free from tenancy rights which works an estoppel as against the Respondent (tenant)",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:485:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,"Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion","(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:486:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,"Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57","(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:487:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-45-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-45,(2001) 3 Sri LR 56,LKCA,2001,In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court,"(i) Since the District Court was of the opinion that this case ought not be disposed of on the issues of law only without any evidence being led, it is not for this Court to invite the District Judge to form a contrary opinion. (ii) Under S.147 C. E C a case to be disposed of as a preliminary issue it should be a pure question of law, which goes to the jurisdiction of the case. Judges of original courts should as far as possible go through the entire trial and answer all the issues unless they are certain that a pure question of law without the leading of evidence can dispose of the case."" 57 (iii) In this case the questions of law are intricately tied up with questions of fact. S. 147 is discretionary in that what is relevant is the opinion of Court",SRC030-s147,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1148:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A party cannot be inconsistent in their approach in legal proceedings, and they cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.","He cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion. The doctrine of approbate and reprobate forbids him from doing so.",SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1606:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A party cannot re-agitate an issue of prescription before a higher court if they failed to appeal against the judgment of the lower court on that issue.,"His contention, therefore, is that the Defendant cannot reagitate the issue of prescription before this Court.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1668:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_168_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"A respondent who has not filed a written objection under Section 772 of the Civil Procedure Code may still be able to resist a decree of a lower Court in the interest of justice, even if the written objection has not been filed.","Hence, it is settled law that, even though a respondent-defendant who has not duly filed a written objection is unable to claim his statutory right to a decree as an entitlement, the Court sitting in appeal has the discretion, by virtue of its inherent jurisdiction, to permit such objection in the interest of justice.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1777:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_23_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"When interpreting a document, all clauses must be considered together, and not just one clause.",Clauses in a document must be interpreted in accordance with other clauses contained in the same document whether they precede or follow it.,SRC030-s147,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1854:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit.,A tenant who disclaims to hold of his landlord and puts him at defiance is not entitled to have the action against him dismissed for want of a valid notice to quit [Muttu Natchia et al v. Patuma Natchia et al (1 NLR 21); Sundra Ammal v. Jusey Appu (36 NLR 400); Pedrick v. Mendis (62 NLR 471); K. Hassen v. A.O.Nagaria (75 NLR 335); Mansoor v. Umma (1984) 1 Sri LR 151; Subramaniam v. Pathmanathan (1984) 1 Sri LR 252; Ranasinghe v. Premadharma (1985) 1 Sri LR 63 at 71].,SRC030-s772,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1980:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_72_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A respondent to an appeal can resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds decided against him in the court below, without filing a written objection under section 772, but can also file a written objection to dispute specific findings in or aspects of the decree.","Thus, where an appellant from a decree entered by an original Civil Court has filed an appeal seeking to set aside or vary that decree: the first limb of section 772 recognises the right of a respondent to that appeal to resist the appeal and support the decree on any grounds including those decided against him by the trial court, without filing a written objection under section 772; and the second limb of section 772 enables a respondent to the appeal who is dissatisfied with some specific finding in or aspect of the decree but has not filed an appeal to canvass it, to dispute that finding or aspect of the decree and seek to have it set aside or varied or decided in his favour by the appellate court, provided he has duly filed a written objection under section 772.",SRC030-s772,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2032:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_89_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A party cannot file a re-vindicatio action against a co-owner without first obtaining a partition of the co-owned property.,Therefore there is no legal bar to allow the Plaintiff to proceed with the case as a re-vindicatio action against the Defendants.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:2118:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_4_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,A defendant who fails to move the court to try an issue as a preliminary issue at the commencement of the trial is deemed to have waived objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of the court to hear and determine the defendant's action.,The defendant has failed to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue. The defendant has failed to formulate a preliminary issue relating to the jurisdiction of the Court at the commencement of the trial. His failure to move Court to try the said issue as a preliminary issue on such a vital matter will amount to a waiver of objections in regard to lack of jurisdiction of Court to hear and determine the defendant’s action.,SRC030-s147,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:26:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-1,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A defendant can be held liable for losses caused to a plaintiff.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:27:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-2,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party cannot claim damages for non-delivery of goods if the delivery orders were issued to unauthorized persons.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:28:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1959-21-3,commonlii-LKCA-1959-21,79 NLR 1,LKCA,1959,A party to a contract may be precluded from relying on an exemption clause if they have committed a fundamental breach of contract.,"(1) That the first defendant was liable for the loss caused to the plaintiff. (2) (Vythialingam, J. dissenting) That though the first defendant's servants issued the delivery orders without production of the Bills of Lading to persons who, to their knowledge, were other than those entitled under the Bills of Lading to receive them, the 2nd defendant is not liable for the non-delivery of the goods as he was protected by the exemption clause in the Bills of Lading. The plaintiff has failed to show any fundamental breach of his contract of affreightment committed-by the 2nd defendant to disentitle him of the benefit of the exemption clause. (3) That a party to a contract may be precluded from relying upon an exemption clause where such parties have been guilty of a fundamental breach of contract or of a breach of a funda�mental term. A fundamental breach disentitling the party from relying on an exemption clause occurs if an act amount�ing to a complete departure from the contract is done which can be imputed to the contracting party himself as opposed to an act for which the contracting party is vicariously res�ponsible as having been committed by one of his servants",SRC030-s772,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:303:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-33-1,commonlii-LKCA-1997-33,(2001) 3 Sri LR 25,LKCA,1997,A Judge should consider the whole case when deciding whether to grant an interlocutory injunction.,"(i) In considering whether to grant an interlocutory injunction the right course for a Judge is to look at the whole Case, he must have regard to not only to the strength of the claim but also the strength of the defence and then decide what is best to be done. The court must be satisfied that there is a serious question to be tried at the hearing and that on the facts before it there is a probability that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. (ii) In this instance when considering the whole case it is seen that there is no probability that the petitioner will succeed in obtaining the reliefs prayed for. It is seen that the Petitioners are the children of the judgment debtor and the deed of conveyance to them by the judgment 26 debtor was on 24. 08. 1993 after the exparte decree was entered against him on 22. 11. 92. Further for 10 months the judgment debtor in 8865/M, the donor on the Deed of Gift did not take any steps to have the exparte decree set aside within a reasonable time. The",SRC030-s247,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:304:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-33-2,commonlii-LKCA-1997-33,(2001) 3 Sri LR 25,LKCA,1997,A petitioner is unlikely to succeed in obtaining reliefs if there is no probability of success.,"(i) In considering whether to grant an interlocutory injunction the right course for a Judge is to look at the whole Case, he must have regard to not only to the strength of the claim but also the strength of the defence and then decide what is best to be done. The court must be satisfied that there is a serious question to be tried at the hearing and that on the facts before it there is a probability that the plaintiff is entitled to relief. (ii) In this instance when considering the whole case it is seen that there is no probability that the petitioner will succeed in obtaining the reliefs prayed for. It is seen that the Petitioners are the children of the judgment debtor and the deed of conveyance to them by the judgment 26 debtor was on 24. 08. 1993 after the exparte decree was entered against him on 22. 11. 92. Further for 10 months the judgment debtor in 8865/M, the donor on the Deed of Gift did not take any steps to have the exparte decree set aside within a reasonable time. The",SRC030-s247,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:305:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-35-1,commonlii-LKCA-1997-35,(1997) 3 Sri LR 202,LKCA,1997,A court cannot try an issue of law that arises from a disputed fact as a preliminary issue.,"(1) As to whether the incident or the facts constituting the cause of action arose on 12.6.1984 or 12.6.1994 is a proposition of fact upon which the parties are at variance on the issues that have been settled and accepted by Court. If an issue of law arises in relation to a fact or factual position in regard to which parties are at variance that issue cannot and ought not to be tried first as a preliminary issue of law. (2) The plaintiff respondent by raising issue (3) on the footing that the relevant date was 12.6.1994 and not 12.6.1984 (date set out in the plaint) must clearly be taken to have abandoned the date given in the plaint and the defendant-petitioner not objecting to such a departure or abandonment must be deemed to have clearly acquiesced on the plaintiff raising the issue giving a new date. If there was no such acquiescence the defendant-petitioner ought to have signified or made his objections known. Silence on the part of the defendant-petitioner when there was a duty to object must be taken to mean not only that the defendant- petitioner did in fact consent to the issue being raised on the basis of 12.6.94 but also the defendant did so consent because no prejudice was occasioned to him thereby. 203 Per Gunawardena, J. ""It also needs to be stressed that in a trial of an action the question as to how or in what manner the issues have to be dealt with or tried is primarily matter best left to the discretion of the trial Judge, and a Court exercising appellate or revisionary powers ought to be slow to interfere with that discretion except perhaps, in a case where it is patent or obvious that the discretion has been exercised by the trial Judge not according to reason but according to caprice.""",SRC030-s147,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:306:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-35-2,commonlii-LKCA-1997-35,(1997) 3 Sri LR 202,LKCA,1997,A plaintiff cannot raise an issue,"(1) As to whether the incident or the facts constituting the cause of action arose on 12.6.1984 or 12.6.1994 is a proposition of fact upon which the parties are at variance on the issues that have been settled and accepted by Court. If an issue of law arises in relation to a fact or factual position in regard to which parties are at variance that issue cannot and ought not to be tried first as a preliminary issue of law. (2) The plaintiff respondent by raising issue (3) on the footing that the relevant date was 12.6.1994 and not 12.6.1984 (date set out in the plaint) must clearly be taken to have abandoned the date given in the plaint and the defendant-petitioner not objecting to such a departure or abandonment must be deemed to have clearly acquiesced on the plaintiff raising the issue giving a new date. If there was no such acquiescence the defendant-petitioner ought to have signified or made his objections known. Silence on the part of the defendant-petitioner when there was a duty to object must be taken to mean not only that the defendant- petitioner did in fact consent to the issue being raised on the basis of 12.6.94 but also the defendant did so consent because no prejudice was occasioned to him thereby. 203 Per Gunawardena, J. ""It also needs to be stressed that in a trial of an action the question as to how or in what manner the issues have to be dealt with or tried is primarily matter best left to the discretion of the trial Judge, and a Court exercising appellate or revisionary powers ought to be slow to interfere with that discretion except perhaps, in a case where it is patent or obvious that the discretion has been exercised by the trial Judge not according to reason but according to caprice.""",SRC030-s147,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:307:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-35-3,commonlii-LKCA-1997-35,(1997) 3 Sri LR 202,LKCA,1997,A party's silence when they have a duty to object to a departure from the original date in a plaint is deemed to be acquiescence and consent to the new date.,"(1) As to whether the incident or the facts constituting the cause of action arose on 12.6.1984 or 12.6.1994 is a proposition of fact upon which the parties are at variance on the issues that have been settled and accepted by Court. If an issue of law arises in relation to a fact or factual position in regard to which parties are at variance that issue cannot and ought not to be tried first as a preliminary issue of law. (2) The plaintiff respondent by raising issue (3) on the footing that the relevant date was 12.6.1994 and not 12.6.1984 (date set out in the plaint) must clearly be taken to have abandoned the date given in the plaint and the defendant-petitioner not objecting to such a departure or abandonment must be deemed to have clearly acquiesced on the plaintiff raising the issue giving a new date. If there was no such acquiescence the defendant-petitioner ought to have signified or made his objections known. Silence on the part of the defendant-petitioner when there was a duty to object must be taken to mean not only that the defendant- petitioner did in fact consent to the issue being raised on the basis of 12.6.94 but also the defendant did so consent because no prejudice was occasioned to him thereby. 203 Per Gunawardena, J. ""It also needs to be stressed that in a trial of an action the question as to how or in what manner the issues have to be dealt with or tried is primarily matter best left to the discretion of the trial Judge, and a Court exercising appellate or revisionary powers ought to be slow to interfere with that discretion except perhaps, in a case where it is patent or obvious that the discretion has been exercised by the trial Judge not according to reason but according to caprice.""",SRC030-s147,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:314:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1997-59-1,commonlii-LKCA-1997-59,(1997) 3 Sri LR 386,LKCA,1997,A possessory action requires an averment of dispossession.,(1) The action is clearly not a possessory action. There is no averment of dispossession as required by section 4 of the Prescriptive Ordinance 22 of 1877. The averments indicate non-possession by plaintiff-appellant for three years and the consequent dispossession. (2) It is clear that the action is also not a Rei vindicatio action. The plaintiff -appellant pleads no title to be declared entitled to the land but to be only a yearly permit holder. (3) The plaintiff-appellant only states that he came to possess on the permit but did not seek a declaration that he was entitled to possess the land on the alleged yearly permit. The consequential relief of the ejectment of the alleged trespasser cannot therefore arise,SRC050-s4,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
