scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1094:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-417-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,"A party cannot be inconsistent in their approach in legal proceedings, they cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.","He cannot blow hot and cold, affirm and disaffirm the same transaction simultaneously to suit the occasion.",SRC030-s772,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_high_confidence.csv:1386:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_40_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"In a rei vindicatio action, the burden of proof is on the defendant to show that he is in lawful possession, once the plaintiff's legal title to the corpus is proved.","Where the legal title to the corpus is proved, the burden of proof is on the Respondent to show that he is in lawful possession [Theivandran v. Ramanathan Chettiar (1986) 2 Sri LR 219 at 222; Wijetunge v. Thangarajah (1999) 1 Sri LR 53 at 55; Candappa Nee Bastian v. Ponnambalampillai (1993) 1 Sri LR 184 at 187; Beebi Johara v. Warusavithana (1998) 3 Sri LR 227 at 228].",SRC030-s772,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
